my fix #100191 is merged yesterday. However, the test Deployment should run the lifecycle of a Deployment still failed twice after that. They have different cauess.

1. Pod failed to start within timeout (1min). 
  - test result: https://prow.k8s.io/view/gs/kubernetes-jenkins/logs/ci-kubernetes-kind-ipv6-e2e-parallel/1375486825459617792
  - Kubelet.log: it shows that after volumes are mounted succesfully, pod is trying to start sandbox, but after 1 min, pod sandbox still not created successfully. This is the same issue I mentioned in comment here https://github.com/kubernetes/kubernetes/issues/100047#issuecomment-799729916. I think this issue only happens in Kind cluster, not gce cluster. @BenTheElder Could you or someone else help investigate?
  
2. Test failed during deployment patch
  - After deployment is patched and scaled down to 1, the logs shows new replicaset is created, and one of the old pod is deleted. 
  - The readyReplica is reached to 1. But in my fix, I added an additional check UnavailableReplicas should be 0. I think somehow this number is not reached to 0 during failed test. Deployment/ReplicaSet has many status including 
  ```
      	// Total number of non-terminated pods targeted by this deployment that have the desired template spec.
	// +optional
	UpdatedReplicas int32

	// Total number of ready pods targeted by this deployment.
	// +optional
	ReadyReplicas int32

	// Total number of available pods (ready for at least minReadySeconds) targeted by this deployment.
	// +optional
	AvailableReplicas int32

	// Total number of unavailable pods targeted by this deployment. This is the total number of
	// pods that are still required for the deployment to have 100% available capacity. They may
	// either be pods that are running but not yet available or pods that still have not been created.
	// +optional
	UnavailableReplicas int32
```
The reason I added UnavailableReplicas is to avoid the problem mentioned in issue https://github.com/kubernetes/kubernetes/issues/100192. Now I am not certain what is the exception of the test, whether the status should be achievable within timeout (1min). Need sig-app help to take a look. @janetkuo 
  
Note: test only flaky in Kind cluster. @BenTheElder @janetkuo @liggitt @msau42

FWIW this would be my decision tree for release-blocker or not:
- have we narrowed this down to a specific set of tests?
  - no, still occurring randomly across tests:
    - has the flake rate reduced since time of filing, and is it acceptably low?
      - no -> still release-blocking
      - yes -> not release-blocking
  - yes, specific set of tests:
    - do these tests exist in the previous release?
      - no, new to this release:
        - has the flake rate reduced since time of filing, and is it acceptably low?
          - no -> still release-blocking
          - yes -> not release-blocking
      - yes, exist in previous release:
        - is the flake rate ~same or lower than the previous release?
          - no -> still release-blocking (we've regressed)
          - yes -> not release-blocking

A few things I would use for data
- https://go.k8s.io/triage shows clusters of test failures over time (not necessarily flakes); it also excludes test failures that didn't cluster with other test failures (cluster is defined by edit distance between normalized failure text)
- http://storage.googleapis.com/k8s-metrics/flakes-latest.json aggregates test flakes per job over the last week (job failed and passed against the same commit), sorted by total number of flakes; consistency is % of commits over last week that did not flake

Messages in https://kubernetes.slack.com/archives/CN0K3TE2C/p1617296486010200 led me to believe it has been narrowed down to a specific set of tests? But I'm not sure.

If it has been, for those tests, then...
- https://go.k8s.io/triage?pr=1&test=the%20lifecycle&xjob=gke&xtest=%5C%5BSlow%5C%5D leads me to believe failure rate hasn't changed meaningfully
